INTRODUCTION
Floods represent the most common form of natural disaster,
which occurs when water overflows and inundates land that is
typically dry. These events are frequently triggered by intense
rainfall, quick melting of snow, or storm surges caused by
tropical cyclones or tsunamis in coastal regions.1 Floods
brought on by catastrophic occurrences (such as intense rains
and tsunamis) as a result of sudden changes in the global
climate can also have an impact on how pollutants are
distributed throughout the environment.2 Some heavy metals
are absorbed by particulate matter and retained in sediments
after entering the body of water.3 Heavy metals are released
into the environment by sediments when hydrodynamic
conditions change (due to dredging or erosion, for example).4
Pollutants that were previously temporarily stored in the
channel are swiftly entrained and moved to the floodplain
during floods.5 The most significant factor in the movement of
