soils and their uptake by plants are of concern because it may
lead to their transfer through the food chain, which can affect
human health.9 Compared to nonleafy vegetables, leafy
vegetables generally have higher and more polluted metal
uptake rates. Because of their higher capacity for absorption
through deposition in contaminated soil, water, and air, leafy
vegetables are hyperaccumulators of harmful heavy metals.10
Chard is a regularly used vegetable in Bosnia and Herzegovina.
Chard (Beta vulgaris subsp. cicla) is a biennial plant typically
cultivated as an annual for its nutrient-rich, edible foliage.
Compared to Spinacia oleracea (true spinach), it exhibits
greater resistance to bolting under elevated temperatures,
making it a valuable alternative for leafy vegetable production
during warmer seasons, when other species are less viable.
Propagation is achieved through clustered seeds, necessitating
early stage thinning to optimize plant spacing. This initial
thinning is suitable for consumption as leafy greens. Harvesting
of mature leaves and petioles generally occurs 50−60 days post

sowing, with the remaining crown capable of producing
subsequent foliage for extended harvesting periods.11
Beta vulgaris is known to uptake heavy metals from soil.12
The amount of metals accessible to biota, also known as the
“bioavailable fraction,” is not represented by a measure of the
overall concentration of metals in the soil.13 Except heavy
metal contamination, flooding has great effect on soil
microbiota.14 Soil oxygen is depleted during flooding, as a
result of microbial activity and water saturation. This results in
decreased oxygen levels as well as changes to the nutrient cycle
and a decrease in soil nitrogen, manganese, iron, and sulfur.15
Analyzing the diversity and abundance of microbial
populations in environmental samples, particularly soil and
water, is essential for understanding the beneficial roles
microorganisms play in supporting the health of plants,
animals, and humans.16,17 Although extensive evidence
supports the influence of carbon availability and soil moisture
on specific microbial populations and ecological processes, the
hypothesis that these factors are primary determinants of the
