gram of dry soil. These microorganisms play a crucial role in
decomposing organic matter, releasing mineral nutrients, and
driving biogeochemical cycling. Additionally, they are involved
in the transformation and degradation of waste materials and
synthetic organic compounds.17,21 The risk of fecal contamination in horticultural soils is a common and significant
microbial food safety concern due to the potential presence of
pathogenic bacteria. Because testing soils for specific pathogens
can be costly and technically complex, fecal indicator bacteria,
such as total coliforms (TC), fecal coliforms, Escherichia coli,
enterococci, and spore-forming bacteria (SFB), are frequently
used to indicate the possible presence of fecal matter and
associated pathogens.22−24 Traditional culture-based methods,
including the spread plate technique and the Most Probable
Number (MPN) method or its modifications, remain
fundamental tools in environmental microbiology. These
approaches offer valuable insights into microbial communities
and support efforts in sustainable soil and water management.
However, the pour plate method may be unsuitable for
cultivating microorganisms that are sensitive to the elevated
temperatures involved in mixing samples with molten agar.15,16
