amounts of heavy metals determined in soil samples is shown
by Table 2.
The average concentrations of heavy metals gained with
aqua regia extraction lie in the following sequence: Mn > Fe >
Pb > Zn > Ni > Cu > Cr > Co > Cd. Among heavy metals,
manganese had the highest average value. Manganese content
falls within the natural range in soil (20−3000 mg/kg).42
When three different sampling subjects are compared, the
sludge is depleted in manganese content. The second most
abundant element was iron. The fact that manganese and iron
are the most abundant is expected for agricultural soils because
they play a significant role regarding soil particulate matter.43
Lead took third place by abundance as a surprise. The average
content of lead in investigated soils (flooded, controls, and

sludge) was above the maximum permissible amount (80 mg/
kg).44 Zinc showed quite uniform concentrations in all three of
the investigated areas. The concentrations of zinc in soils (17−
125 mg/kg) presented by Kabata-Pendias44,45 are applicable to
the results of this research. In one case, nickel showed higher
concentrations than permissible (35 mg/kg).46 Chromium,
cobalt, and cadmium showed lower concentrations than
maximum permissible.46−48 The results showed that sediments
transported by floodwaters were high in Pb, Cu, Co, and Fe. In
terms of potentially harmful exposure to trace metals, only the
levels of Pb seem to be alarming. Even minimal amounts of Pb
can significantly jeopardize human health. Pb has several toxic
effects in both humans and animals, including interference with
hemoglobin synthesis, gastrointestinal disorders, and damage
to the central nervous system.49 Sludge was depleted in Mn,
Ni, Zn, and Cr content. The depletion of certain heavy metals
is determined by the change in redox conditions50
