The average concentrations of heavy metals gained with
EDTA extraction lie in the following sequence: Pb > Mn > Fe
> Cu > Zn > Ni > Co > Cr > Cd. All investigated heavy metals
had much lower available concentrations. The decreasing trend
of extractability is presented in the following sequence: Cu >
Pb > Zn > Ni > Fe > Co > Mn > Cr > Cd.
In the case of cadmium, there were no detectable amounts
by means of AAS. Copper showed the highest extractability
with EDTA compared to aqua regia (average: 46.53%).
Copper is in a large portion present as easily extractable
fractions. A similar result (50% extractability) was reported by
Manouchehri et al. and Di Palma and Medici.27,51 Straightforward Cu extraction methods commonly utilized in soil analysis,
like total Cu or EDTA-extractable Cu measurements, offer
sufficient indicators of Cu bioavailability for specific soil
types.52 Interestingly, lead was also highly extractable (average:
18.7%), which means that lead is partially present as soluble
fractions, which form complexes with EDTA.53 EDTA has high
binding affinity toward Pb.27,54 Instead of EDTA, some other
complexing agents can be used to assess the bioavailability of

heavy metals. To enhance the solubility of heavy metals in soil,
EDTA is the most effective chelator for Pb, DPTA and EDTA
are more suitable for Zn and Cd, and EDDS, EDTA, and
DTPA are effective for Cu.55
The average concentrations of heavy metals with two
different extraction procedures in soil samples are presented in
Figures 2 and 3.
The highest concentration of iron was found in the sludge
and the lowest in the flooded area. Sludge was depleted in
manganese content and richer in lead content. Floods brought
significantly higher amounts of iron and lead than was present
as background (controls). The level of contamination with lead
could be increased by precipitation of the sludge after flooding.
The sludge, after flooding, showed a decrease in the content
of zinc, nickel, and chromium. This suggests that the flooding
process may have led to leaching, dilution, or redistribution of
these metals. Conversely, the sludge became enriched in
copper and cobalt, indicating that these metals either

