physicochemical properties, including water retention, aggregation, pH buffering, and cation exchange capacity, all of which
are influenced by the microbial community structure and
activity.75,76 According to Wilson et al.,71 flooding leads to a
rapid increase in carbon turnover and microbial activity,
characterized by enhanced carbon mineralization, enzyme
production, and substrate utilization, likely driven by
opportunistic bacteria. Within 3 to 7 days post flooding,
carbon availability increased, enzyme activity peaked, and
microbial substrate diversity declined. By day 7, the first
detectable shifts in the microbial community composition were
observed.
Plant Material Analysis. Heavy Metals in the Plant
Material. Because of the substantial variation in heavy-metal
concentrations, the results for the plant material are presented
separately in Figures 7 and 8.
In most cases, zinc and manganese had the highest
concentrations in the leaves, while the concentration of iron
varied significantly. The iron concentration in most cases was
the highest in roots and less in green tissues such as leaves and
stems. The analysis of iron concentrations across various
organs or components of more than 1000 plant species has
revealed significant variations both between and within species,
with few identifiable patterns, if any.77 Zinc, manganese, and
iron are essential micronutrients for plant health, impacting

