concentration was higher in the roots. After being absorbed
through soil solutions, Pb-ions primarily gather in the roots
because of the barriers created by Casparian strips in the root
endodermis.79
Copper, nickel, and cobalt were higher in leaves in most
cases. Copper and nickel are essential metals for plant growth,
and cobalt is potentially essential. The higher concentrations of
these metals in the leaves suggest that they may play an
important role in plant metabolism or may be actively
transported to the leaves for physiological processes, such as
photosynthesis or enzyme activation. The presence of cobalt in
the leaves might also indicate that it serves a functional, though
less well-defined, role in plant health.80 Cobalt is potentially
essential.81 Chromium was higher in roots, while cadmium
varied among different parts of the chard. Chromium is toxic to
plants, and it is mainly accumulated within the roots.82 The
variability of the cadmium distribution across different parts of
the chard may suggest that cadmium uptake and translocation
within the plant are more complex. Its distribution could be
influenced by environmental factors, plant age, or even specific
mechanisms of detoxification of cadmium in various tissues.
